  "image_name": "0ef591758138cc375197ec7d3d623f3c.png",
  "prompt_text": "This figure summarizes how infection in pregnancy can reach the fetus and what consequences it can have for the mother, placenta, and fetus. On the left, a cross-section of the pregnant uterus shows several possible routes of spread: infection can cross the placenta into the fetal circulation, pass across the fetal membranes, or ascend from the lower genital tract. The placenta is highlighted as a major interface, with visible signs of local damage such as placental disruption and fetal–maternal hemorrhage.\n\nOn the right, the pregnant body is paired with a smaller uterine cross-section and text boxes that group the clinical effects by target. For the mother, the listed outcomes emphasize systemic illness and inflammation, including sepsis, respiratory distress, obstetric hemorrhage, and even death. For the placenta, the figure points to impaired nutrient and oxygen transport, the possibility of serving as a reservoir for reinfection, and preterm labor. For the fetus, the consequences appear broad and severe, including growth restriction, congenital abnormalities, sensory deficits such as blindness or deafness, microcephaly or later neurodevelopmental problems, bone marrow suppression, and fetal demise.\n\nOverall, the figure conveys that infection during pregnancy can spread through multiple anatomical pathways and cause serious harm at several levels simultaneously.",
